#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198045 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198403 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd9aad0a-f415-577c-9cb0-15044e4a43fa" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux in a Source-Free Vector Field"}] :question/id #uuid "7a42fb45-176b-5550-a206-41a92859987b" :question/math-academy-id "e-12440" :question/problem "![](images/f9/f9b1b286210d694cd3786fe38190216da503bfa3223114f86c8706f810313e33.png)\n\nThe curve $C_1$ has endpoints $A(5,3)$ and $B(1,3).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\sqrt{y^3}\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\sqrt{(5-x)^3}\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\n through $C_1$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector  $\\mathbf{n}_1,$ as shown above. \n\n*Hint: You're given that $\\mathbf F$ is source-free.*" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q\\,\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2,$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $\\mathbf{F}$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F = \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} = 0.$\n\n- The flux $\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s}$ along any closed curve $C \\in D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves $C_1, C_2 \\in D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}  \\,\\textrm{d}s\n=\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}  \\,\\textrm{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $\\psi(x,y)$ for $\\mathbf{F}.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(\\sqrt{y^3}\\right) + \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(\\sqrt{(5-x)^3}\\right) = 0+0 = 0,\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $\\mathbf{F}$ is a source-free vector field.\n\nSince $\\mathbf{F}$ is source-free, we know the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ is independent of the path. In particular,\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_1 \\,\\textrm{d}s\n=\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_2 \\,\\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $C_2$ is the straight line from $A(5,3)$ to $B(1,3)$ and $\\mathbf{n}_2$ is its unit normal, shown in the diagram below. \n\n![](images/2a/2aeb26e58251923821d4058f886eada6a29b473ea70714b32e98c5338ddd0b28.png)\n\nNotice that both $\\mathbf n_1$ and $\\mathbf n_2$ are $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotations of their respective unit tangent vectors.\n\nTo calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C_2,$ we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_2 \\,\\textrm{d}s  = \\int\\limits_{C_2} P\\,\\textrm{d}y - Q\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply the formula,\n\n- the curve should be parametrized as $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t \\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $\\mathbf{n}_2$ *must* be a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation of the unit normal tangent vector $\\mathbf{T}(t).$\n\nParametrizing $C_2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =5\\,i+3\\,j+t\\cdot [(i+3\\,j)-(5\\,i+3\\,j)] \\\\  & =(5-4t)\\,i+3\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in [0,1].$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}=\\sqrt{(3)^{3}}=3\\sqrt{3}, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =\\sqrt{(5-x)^{3}}=\\sqrt{(5-(5-4t))^{3}}=8\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5-4t)=-4, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the flux integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3\\sqrt{3})\\cdot (0)-(8\\sqrt{{t}^{3}})\\cdot (-4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}32{t}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{64{t}^{5/2}}{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{64(1)^{5/2}}{5})-(\\frac{(64(0)^{5/2}}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_1\\, \\textrm{d}s = \\dfrac{64}{5}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198242 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198368 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2dcf4be-ea5a-5ab3-8919-e61da330fa85" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Source-Free Vector Fields"}] :question/id #uuid "366c826f-6f15-5a21-9eaa-d74b6e4d8833" :question/math-academy-id "e-9575" :question/problem "Given the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = 4y(x^2+y^2)\\,\\mathbf{i} - 4x(x^2+y^2)\\,\\mathbf{j},$ which of the following statements must be true?\n\nI. $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F= 0$\n\nII. $\\mathbf{F}$ is a source-free vector field\n\nIII. For two arbitrary curves $C_1$ and $C_2,$ we have $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s \\neq \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s.$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q\\,\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2,$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $\\mathbf{F}$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F = \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} = 0.$\n\n- The flux $\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s}$ along any closed curve $C \\in D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves $C_1,C_2 \\in D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}  \\,\\textrm{d}s\n=\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}  \\,\\textrm{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $\\psi(x,y)$ for $\\mathbf{F}.$\n\nNote that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)$ is a vector field with the simply connected domain $D=\\mathbb{R}^2,$ where \n\n$$\nP(x,y) = 4y(x^2+y^2),\\qquad Q(x,y) = - 4x(x^2+y^2).\n$$\n\nComputing the partial derivatives, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=8xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-8xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\n=\n8xy + (-8xy) = 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. This follows from statement I.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\mathbf F$ is source-free, we have that $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s$ for all curves *with the same start and endpoints*.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198612 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198403 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd9aad0a-f415-577c-9cb0-15044e4a43fa" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux in a Source-Free Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b20b10fc-bb64-57ca-9ec9-e4d2df501a0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194304} {:answer/id #uuid "c439e078-219a-5d7c-931d-f4512f82a95f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194305} {:answer/id #uuid "c5449f6b-066e-5959-8004-2b1d283f4af0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194306} {:answer/id #uuid "f048743c-1904-5a19-bb5a-ec041bc93630" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194307} {:answer/id #uuid "36e11e32-f25b-529c-abe5-51889402664c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194308}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f048743c-1904-5a19-bb5a-ec041bc93630" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194307} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d966562-9c87-57b1-a8bf-6b3edf2422f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194303}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bdff88fa-fe4d-5059-a512-6bd7bc96d899" :question/math-academy-id "q-161439" :question/problem "![](images/07/07aaf788552b002f804b98558a79a2951ac091897539093bbffc0063a2289897.png)\n\nThe curve ${C}_{1}$ has endpoints $A(1,-1)$ and $B(-1,1).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=x\\,i+(1-y)\\,j$ through ${C}_{1}$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector ${n}_{1},$ as shown above.\n\n*Hint: You're given that $F$ is source-free, and the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B$ can be parametrized as*\n\n$$\nr=(1-2t)\\,i+(-1+2t)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s$ along any closed curve $C\\in D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}\\in D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-y)=1-1=0,\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\nSince $F$ is source-free, we know the flux of $F$ is independent of the path. In particular, as we have that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{2}$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B,$ and ${n}_{2}$ is the unit normal to ${C}_{2},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/e2/e22757cf7c4e2be17cb71e63c550aa39a10ee1dc5895c63acd5a45f0baa94623.png)\n\nNotice that both ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotations of their respective unit tangent vectors.\n\nTo calculate the flux of $F$ across ${C}_{2},$ we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply the formula,\n\n- the curve should be parametrized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector ${n}_{2}$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit normal tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nWe have the parametrization\n\n$$\nr(t)=(1-2t)\\,i+(-1+2t)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =x=1-2t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =1-y=1-(2t-1)=2-2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-2t)=-2, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-1)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the flux integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-2t)\\cdot (2)-(2-2t)\\cdot (-2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-2t)+(2-2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}3-4t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\cdot {[3t-2{t}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (3-2) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435336 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198368 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2dcf4be-ea5a-5ab3-8919-e61da330fa85" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Source-Free Vector Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08735882-9207-5309-a7e8-afc080bb5d20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435316} {:answer/id #uuid "0d718fae-7a62-50b9-a0aa-cf9733addd96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Not enough information" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435317} {:answer/id #uuid "511b422f-6da0-5ba2-afb2-87e7a51ca4a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435324} {:answer/id #uuid "64e7b7f1-9e0e-5777-8160-d11edf79c8d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435325} {:answer/id #uuid "a29a253a-123d-5aee-b6ed-f838b5093d69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435328}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d718fae-7a62-50b9-a0aa-cf9733addd96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Not enough information" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435317} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f879c13-dbbc-55d7-b911-a1fc2b3863d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435332}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dd61f71d-b16d-557d-a304-ee717f93d77f" :question/math-academy-id "q-162554" :question/problem "![](images/62/62946451fd0305439366bdc22db84f8a5823481082e8ac39858369fbc582676d.png)\n\nWhat is the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,i+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j$ across the curve $C$ shown above?\n\n*Hint: Start by checking whether $F$ is source-free.*" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s$ along any closed curve $C⊂D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}⊂D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\  & =(1+x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $F$ is *not* a source-free vector field. \n\nSince $F$ is not source-free, we do not have enough information to determine the flux of $F$ through $C.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435335 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198368 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2dcf4be-ea5a-5ab3-8919-e61da330fa85" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Source-Free Vector Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12950e32-6502-5362-9826-8bea8d1040b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435320} {:answer/id #uuid "2952bfeb-9ffe-59c6-8382-80bb73507c7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435322} {:answer/id #uuid "4986aec7-0bc6-58da-840b-ace2ec7bef03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435323} {:answer/id #uuid "8ef61b54-efe4-55bd-bf3c-33b3e7236ba5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435326} {:answer/id #uuid "9f404acf-6d24-59ae-b9e4-103a63b7dfe2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435327}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f404acf-6d24-59ae-b9e4-103a63b7dfe2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435327} :answer-field/id #uuid "984d6c5b-f37c-575f-adde-0cd67c2d7720" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435333}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "568651db-e544-5f07-84be-3a27fc735107" :question/math-academy-id "q-162558" :question/problem "Given the vector field $F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i+2x{y}^{2}\\,j,$ which of the following statements must be true?\n\nI. $∇\\cdot F=0$\n\nII. There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F$\n\nIII. There exists a close curve $C$ such that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s\\ne 0$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s$ along any closed curve $C⊂D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}⊂D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nNote that $F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i+2x{y}^{2}\\,j$ is a vector field with the simply connected domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P(x,y)={x}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=2x{y}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=2x+4xy=2x(1+2y)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Indeed, they follow from the fact that $∇\\cdot F\\ne 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435334 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198403 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd9aad0a-f415-577c-9cb0-15044e4a43fa" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux in a Source-Free Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0de951e4-d86b-5202-9ac2-026b4bcf6c5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435318} {:answer/id #uuid "0e2fa68f-c7f0-5f8d-9ba5-b91308434c7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435319} {:answer/id #uuid "1670c8fd-d03b-5200-a07e-b492d22de093" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435321} {:answer/id #uuid "cc9da38b-fae8-51bd-ae43-39acaa1e089a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435329} {:answer/id #uuid "df4b2841-2e6a-5e02-b6d1-1b6fce984f1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435330}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e2fa68f-c7f0-5f8d-9ba5-b91308434c7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435319} :answer-field/id #uuid "19bee987-1ec3-5c8c-ac5a-92a635a303b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435331}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "21126858-0865-5434-a374-dbf17e23e496" :question/math-academy-id "q-162590" :question/problem "![](images/5f/5fa49c55e5741ccf46a2c77dd5e1770a8e5e7b89a29641af44e42eecbbb82f17.png)\n\nThe curve ${C}_{1}$ has endpoints $A(2,-1)$ and $B(-1,1).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=(2x+y)\\,i+(-2y)\\,j$ through ${C}_{1}$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector ${n}_{1},$ as shown above.\n\n*Hint: You're given that $F$ is source-free, and the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B$ can be parametrized as*\n\n$$\nr=(-3t+2)\\,i+(2t-1)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s$ along any closed curve $C\\in D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}\\in D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2y)=2-2=0,\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\nSince $F$ is source-free, we know the flux of $F$ is independent of the path. In particular, as we have that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{2}$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B,$ and ${n}_{2}$ is the unit normal to ${C}_{2},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/6b/6bb6e38f83006fa9874ca0020e4eb016ea8eed7fec79596e984b94a0f3853d75.png)\n\nNotice that both ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotations of their respective unit tangent vectors.\n\nTo calculate the flux of $F$ across ${C}_{2},$ we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply the formula,\n\n- the curve should be parametrized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector ${n}_{2}$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit normal tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nWe have the parametrization\n\n$$\nr(t)=(-3t+2)\\,i+(2t-1)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =2x+y=2(-3t+2)+(2t-1)=-4t+3, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =-2y=-2(2t-1)=-4t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-3t+2)=-3, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-1)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the flux integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-4t+3)\\cdot (2)-(-4t+2)\\cdot (-3)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-8t+6)+(6-12t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(12-20t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[12t-10{t}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(12-10) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s=2.\n$$"}]}
